Now yes. I connected the LM word-level path to the same earningscall fetch flow as the existing app.

What I changed:
- `src/loughran_mcdonald.py`:
  - added `transcript_to_lm_records()`, which fetches a transcript through the same fetcher path, splits it with the existing `TranscriptPreprocessor`, and scores every sentence with the LM scorer
  - made the scorer easier to reuse by storing the label schema on the instance instead of a global constant
  - cleaned up the empty-transcript branch so the helper always returns a complete record shape
- `eval_lm_word_level.py`:
  - rewrote it cleanly
  - kept the built-in smoke test
  - added a fetched-transcript mode using `--ticker`, `--year`, and `--quarter`
  - prints the same style of output as the rest of the project: ticker, year, quarter, sentence count, schema, a sample of sentence-level scores, and aggregated category shares
- `requirements.txt`:
  - added `spacy>=3.5.0` because the transcript path now uses the existing SpaCy preprocessor
- `README.md`:
  - documented the fetched-transcript usage for the LM script

Verification I ran:
- `python -c "import src.loughran_mcdonald, eval_lm_word_level"` now imports cleanly
- I also installed `earningscall` and `spacy` and downloaded `en_core_web_sm` because the transcript path depends on them
- `transcript_to_lm_records()` returns the expected keys and produces real LM category scores for a short test sentence

A few important notes:
- this path still requires `spacy` and `en_core_web_sm`, so the fetched-transcript mode will not work if those are missing
- the fetcher itself is unchanged, so the LM option uses the same `earningscall` behavior as the off-the-shelf model; the difference is only in how the text is scored afterward
- the LM scorer does not need a GPU, but the fetcher and SpaCy preprocessor still run locally

If you want, the next step is to decide whether this should also appear inside the Streamlit app or stay as a separate script. A good follow-up would be either:
- add the LM word-level scoring panel to `app.py` with the same ticker/year/quarter inputs
- or keep it script-only and add a small batch mode that scores several tickers and saves the results somewhere
